.TH PSF-LINUX-KERNEL-LPE-CHECK 8 "30 October 2026" "psf-security-patches 26.10.30-1.0.0" "System Administration"
.ad l
.SH NAME
.ad l
psf-linux-kernel-lpe-check \- Linux kernel local root quartet: checker
.SH SYNOPSIS
.ad l
\fB/\:\:opt/\:\:psf\-security\-patches/\:\:linux\-kernel/\:\:linux\-kernel\-lpe\-check.sh\fR [check]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.ad l
.PP
DirtyAH6 CVE-2026-80844, TUNderflow CVE-2026-81000, PPPoEject CVE-2026-68121,
.PP
DiagSpill CVE-2026-74469
.PP
Four local privilege escalation flaws in the Linux kernel, disclosed on the oss-security list
on 18 September 2026 by Asim Manizada, after an embargo agreed with the linux-distros list
(reported to the kernel security team in mid-July). Public proof-of-concept exploits exist.
Each gives an unprivileged local user code execution as root on the systems it targets, and
each can corrupt the host kernel from a container. The bugs are 10 to 21 years old.
.RS 2
.nf
CVE-2026-80844  DirtyAH6    IPsec AH over IPv6 (ah6): ipv6_rearrange_rthdr() does not check
                            that segments_left is within the
                              header, an out-of-bounds
                            access. Needs unprivileged user and
                              network namespaces, or
                            CAP_NET_ADMIN and CAP_NET_RAW over an
                              attacker-controlled
                            network namespace. Remote crash only
                              if the host routes IPv6
                            and adds an Authentication Header in
                              transport mode.
CVE-2026-81000  TUNderflow  TUN/TAP: oversized receive headroom
  underflows a length and
                            leaves skb->data beyond its
                              allocation. Needs unprivileged
                            user and network namespaces, or
                              CAP_NET_ADMIN over one.
CVE-2026-68121  PPPoEject   PPPoE: pppoe_sendmsg() keeps a pointer into a socket-buffer
                            head that dev_hard_header() can free
                              (use after free). Needs
                            unprivileged user and network
                              namespaces, or CAP_NET_ADMIN.
CVE-2026-74469  DiagSpill   SCTP: a 16-bit peer transport count wraps to zero, so sctp_diag
                            copies about 8 MiB past the end of its
                              Netlink reply. Needs
                            SCTP and sctp_diag, and NO user
                              namespace or capability.
                            Remote crash only with non-default
                              SCTP options.
.fi
.RE
.RS 2
.nf
Affected: DirtyAH6 and PPPoEject since 2.6.12, TUNderflow since
  4.6, DiagSpill since 4.7,
          through the stable releases below. Every release of a
            branch not listed in the
          table (5.11 to 5.14, 5.16 to 6.0, 6.2 to 6.5, 6.7 to
            6.11, 6.13 to 6.17, 6.19
          to 7.0, and older ones such as 5.4) is affected and has
            no upstream fix.
First fixed stable release of each branch, per flaw (DirtyAH6 /
  TUNderflow / PPPoEject /
DiagSpill):
  5.10  269 / 270 / 265 / 265      6.12  108 / 109 / 101 / 103
  5.15  220 / 221 / 216 / 216      6.18   49 /  50 /  42 /  44
  6.1   187 / 188 / 183 / 183      7.1    13 /  14 /   6 /   8
  6.6   156 / 157 / 148 / 151      7.2     3 /   4 / not affected / not affected
The first stable releases with all four fixes: 5.10.270, 5.15.221,
  6.1.188, 6.6.157,
6.12.109, 6.18.50 and 7.2.4.
.fi
.RE
.PP
Why this script reads the kernel's origin first. The upstream patch level is in the release
string only on kernels that follow the stable series (vanilla, Arch, Alpine, most custom
builds). Debian puts it in `uname \-v`. Ubuntu, RHEL and its derivatives, SUSE and Amazon Linux
backport fixes under release strings that do not say which fixes they carry, so a number from
them cannot be compared with the table. For those the vendor changelog is the evidence: the
script looks in it for each CVE, and points you to the vendor's tracker when it is absent. A kernel that
is a stable release candidate (\-rc) is judged as the release before it.
.PP
What the exposure lines mean. They report whether the module each flaw needs is loaded,
built in or loadable, and whether unprivileged user namespaces are allowed. That is
information for you to weigh, not a verdict: a kernel below its fixed release is affected
whatever these show, and a process with the right capabilities, such as a container, does not
need a user namespace for the first three. That reading is the project's.
.PP
A container shows the host's kernel, so run this on the host.
.PP
GitHub proof-of-concept repositories exist for all four, so there is no indicator to hunt
for in logs and this script has no hunt. It is read-only.
.SH COMMANDS AND OPTIONS
.ad l
.TP
\fBcheck (default)\fR
.ad l
Exit 0 = the running kernel has all four fixes (or vendor backports),
1 = affected by at least one, or a vendor backport could not be confirmed.
.TP
\fBmitigate [\-\-userns] [\-\-modules LIST] [\-\-force] [\-\-dry\-run]\fR
.ad l
(root) changes the host.
The researcher's protective measures; a patched kernel is the
permanent fix: they only act when check finds
the kernel affected (\-\-force overrides). Choose what to apply,
because each can break software that depends on it:
.RS 2
.TP 10
\fB\-\-userns\fR
.ad l
stops unprivileged user namespaces, using the mildest switch this kernel has: kernel.unprivileged_userns_clone=0, else kernel.apparmor_restrict_unprivileged_userns=1 (restricts, does not fully block). Only with \-\-force does it fall back to user.max_user_namespaces=0, which stops ALL new user namespaces. Breaks rootless containers (podman, rootless docker), flatpak and browser sandboxes. The change is applied live and written to a sysctl.d file. It does not stop DiagSpill, which needs no namespace.
.TP 10
\fB\-\-modules LIST\fR
.ad l
prevents loading of the listed modules, from: ah6 tun pppoe sctp sctp_diag ebt_snat (or all). "install NAME /bin/false" lines in a modprobe.d file. A module that is loaded now is left alone (it cannot be unloaded safely here) unless \-\-force, which still only blocks later loads. tun is used by OpenVPN, Tailscale and QEMU networking; sctp by telecom software; pppoe by DSL dial-up; ah6 by IPsec AH; ebt_snat by bridge firewalls.
.RE
\-\-dry\-run shows everything and changes nothing. Previous sysctl
values are saved so that rollback can put them back.
.TP
\fBrollback [\-\-dry\-run]\fR
.ad l
restore the saved sysctl values and remove the files mitigate wrote.
.TP
\fBversion\fR
.ad l
print the script version.
.PP
Env: KERNEL_CHANGELOG   a vendor changelog file to read, plain or .gz
.RS 2
.nf
STATE_DIR (default /\:var/\:lib/\:psf\-security\-patches/\:linux\-kernel),
  SYSCTL_D and
MODPROBE_D (default /etc/sysctl.d and /etc/modprobe.d) for
  mitigate and rollback
KERNEL_RELEASE, KERNEL_VERSION_STRING   override uname \-r and uname \-v
KERNEL_MODULES_FILE, KERNEL_PROC_SYS    override /proc/modules and /proc/sys
KERNEL_BUILTIN_FILE   override /lib/modules/<release>/modules.builtin
.fi
.RE
.SH FILES
.ad l
.TP
\fB/\:\:opt/\:\:psf\-security\-patches/\:\:linux\-kernel/\:\:linux\-kernel\-lpe\-check.sh\fR
.ad l
The script.
.TP
\fB/\:var/\:lib/\:psf\-security\-patches/\:linux\-kernel\fR
.ad l
Backups and the record that rollback uses.
